CHAPTERS = [
dict(title="A Foggy Evening in London", scene="fogstreet", paras=[
"Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive. Tonight we are going back to London, on a November evening around the year eighteen eighty, when a thick, soft, yellow fog has settled over the whole city. Nothing dramatic is going to happen. You will simply walk home through the fog after a long day, stopping now and then, until you reach a warm room and a fire.",
"Before we set out, let your body settle into wherever you are lying. Let your shoulders drop away from your ears. Take one slow breath in, as if you were breathing the cool, damp evening air, and then breathe out for a little longer than you breathed in. Once more, slowly. Good. That is all the effort tonight will ask of you.",
"If you lose the thread of the story, that is perfectly all right. The fog is thick, and it is easy to lose your way in it. Just let the voice carry on beside you like a quiet companion, and if you fall asleep somewhere along the walk, you will still arrive home safely in the end.",
]),

dict(title="The Fog Comes Down", scene="embankment", paras=[
"It is a little after five o'clock, and you are standing on the top step of an office near the Strand, buttoning your coat. Behind you, the clerks are covering their ledgers and turning down the gas. In front of you, the street has almost disappeared. The fog came creeping up from the river in the middle of the afternoon, and now it fills the whole road like milky tea poured into a cup.",
"Londoners have many names for a fog like this. Some call it a pea-souper, because it is thick and yellowish green, like a bowl of split pea soup. Others call it a London particular. Charles Dickens used that phrase in his novel Bleak House, when a young clerk explains to a visitor from the country that the fog outside is simply a London particular, as if it were a local speciality.",
"The fog is not only made of water. London in eighteen eighty is a city of several million people, and nearly every house burns coal. On a cold, still evening, the smoke from all those chimneys has nowhere to go. It sinks and mingles with the river mist, and turns the air the colour of weak mustard, faintly sooty, with a smell of coal fires and wet stone.",
"On days like this, the gas in the shop windows is sometimes lit at noon, and stays lit until closing time. The shopkeepers along the Strand have been working all afternoon by its light, and their windows glow softly as you pass, full of gloves and umbrellas and books and pastries, each one a little lit stage floating in the dusk.",
"You step down into it, and the first thing you notice is the quiet. Fog swallows sound. The wheels of the carts and the hooves of the horses, which usually make the Strand a roaring place, are softened now to a muffled clopping, as if the whole city had put on felt slippers. Voices come out of the fog close by, and then fade away again before you can see the faces.",
"You turn down a narrow lane towards the river, because the walk along the water is the gentlest way home. The walls on either side are damp and black with soot. A cat sits on a window sill, tucked into itself, watching you pass with half-closed eyes. Ahead, at the bottom of the lane, there is a soft glow, where the lamps along the river are already lit.",
"Then you hear it, faint and far away, coming through the fog from the west. It is the great clock at Westminster, chiming the quarter hour. Its four slow notes drift over the water, softened and blurred, as if someone were playing them under a blanket. Many people in this city set their evening by those chimes, and tonight you will hear them more than once on your walk.",
"The clock tower is only about twenty years old in eighteen eighty, and the great bell inside it, which everyone already calls Big Ben, has a crack in it, so its voice is a little deeper and rougher than it was meant to be. At night, the four faces of the clock are lit from behind, so that they glow like pale moons above the river. Tonight, in the fog, you cannot see them at all.",
"At the bottom of the lane you come out onto the river wall. The fog here is a little thinner, moving in slow banks over the water, and the long line of lamps along the embankment fades away into it, each one a little fainter than the one before, until the last ones are only soft smudges of gold. You stop for a moment, and simply stand and look.",
]),

dict(title="The Lamplighter's Round", scene="fogstreet", paras=[
"Turning away from the river for a little while, you walk up a side street of tall brick houses, and you meet the lamplighter on his round. He is a thin man in a cap and a long coat, with a ladder on his shoulder for the lamps that need cleaning, and in his hand a long pole with a little flame burning at its tip, like a candle on a stick.",
"London has been lit by gas for more than sixty years by now. Under the pavements runs a network of iron pipes, carrying gas from the great gasworks by the river, where the coal is baked in hot ovens and the gas is stored in huge round holders that rise and sink as they fill and empty. From there it travels quietly under the streets, to tens of thousands of lamps.",
"The lamplighter stops beneath a lamp post, reaches up with his pole, and turns the little tap inside the glass. Then he touches the flame to the burner, and with a soft pop the lamp comes alive, a small fan of yellow light inside the glass box. In clear weather it would throw a neat circle of light onto the pavement. Tonight the fog turns it into a great round halo.",
"He walks on to the next lamp without hurrying. He has done this round every evening for years, in rain, in snow and in fog, and his feet know the way even when his eyes cannot see it. He knows every lamp on the round as if it were an old friend. At dawn he will walk the same streets again, turning them all out, one by one.",
"His work does not end with lighting and putting out. In the daytime he climbs his ladder to polish the glass panes of each lamp, trims the burners, and replaces any glass broken by a careless cart. A clean lamp gives a better light, he will tell you, and in weather like this, every little bit of light is a kindness to someone finding their way home.",
"Behind him, the street is slowly filled with light, lamp after lamp, like a string of pearls being laid down in the fog. Children used to follow lamplighters on their rounds, just to watch them work, and a small boy is doing it now, trotting a few steps behind with his hands in his pockets, until his mother calls him in from a doorway.",
"The houses along this street are tall and narrow, built of brick that was once yellow and is now dark grey with soot. Each one has the same front door with a half-moon of glass above it, called a fanlight, and the same flight of steps, and the same iron railings in front, guarding a narrow space that drops down to the kitchen windows below the street.",
"In one of those kitchens, below the railings, you can see a cook moving about in the lamplight, lifting the lid of a pan, and the warm smell of onions and gravy drifts up through the fog to the pavement. Somewhere above, a curtain is drawn across a window, and the shadow of someone reaching up to close it moves softly behind the blind.",
"At the corner stands a red pillar box, its paint shining wet in the lamplight. Letters put in it tonight will be collected late in the evening by a postman with a lantern, carried through the fog to the sorting office, and delivered across London by the morning. In this city, there are several posts a day, and a letter written after breakfast can often be answered before supper.",
]),

dict(title="Along the River Wall", scene="embankment", paras=[
"You make your way back down to the river, and walk west along the embankment. This broad, quiet road beside the water is still quite new in eighteen eighty. Only ten years ago, the river here ran up against mud banks and old wharves, and at low tide the smell was terrible. Then the engineers built this great granite wall out into the water, and filled in the space behind it.",
"The man behind it was Joseph Bazalgette, the engineer of London's new sewers. Hidden under your feet, inside the embankment, there is a great brick tunnel carrying the city's waste away downstream, and beside it, a railway tunnel, where steam trains run underground. Above it all is this wide road, with young plane trees along it, and a promenade beside the river, where people can walk in the air.",
"All along the top of the river wall, at regular intervals, there are cast iron lamp posts, and at the foot of each one two plump iron fish curl their tails around the column. People call them the dolphin lamps, though they look a little like sturgeon. They were designed for the embankment by an architect named George Vulliamy, and tonight each one wears a crown of misty golden light.",
"Not everyone along this stretch of river trusts gas any longer. For the last couple of years, part of the embankment has been lit as an experiment with a new kind of lamp, an electric one, which burns with a hard, bluish white glare. People come down in the evening just to look at it. But it is costly, and most of London still prefers the gentle yellow of the gas.",
"Below the lamps, set into the face of the granite wall just above the water, there are great bronze lion heads, each one holding a heavy mooring ring in its mouth. Boats can tie up to them. The story goes, among the watermen, that if the river ever rises high enough for the lions to drink, London will be in flood. Tonight the tide is low, and the lions are very dry.",
"A little further along, standing on its own by the river wall, there is something very old and very strange to find in London. It is a tall stone needle, an obelisk, carved in Egypt more than three thousand years ago. People call it Cleopatra's Needle, though it is far older than Cleopatra. It was raised here only two years ago, in the autumn of eighteen seventy eight.",
"When it was set up, a time capsule was buried in its base, filled with things that might one day tell people what life in London was like. There are newspapers and coins, a railway guide, a box of hairpins, a baby's feeding bottle, a set of children's toys, a razor, and photographs of the day. You think about them lying there in the dark, under the old pink stone, as you walk past.",
"Out on the river, the fog lies low over the water, and through it you can hear the quiet sounds of the Thames at night. Water slaps gently against the wall. Somewhere a chain rattles softly as a barge settles against its mooring. Then the low, puffing breath of a little steam tug comes out of the fog, and you see its lit wheelhouse slide slowly past, like a lantern carried on the water.",
"Ahead of you, across the river to the left, there is the dim shape of a long bridge, with the lamps along it making a faint curve of gold in the air. A train is crossing it, very slowly, and you can hear the soft beat of its engine and see the glow from its firebox. Then it is gone into the fog on the other side, and the river is quiet again.",
]),

dict(title="The Cabmen's Shelter", scene="cabstand", paras=[
"Where the embankment meets a wider street, there is a cab rank, a line of hansom cabs waiting along the kerb, their lamps glowing in the fog. A hansom is a light, two-wheeled carriage, with room for two passengers inside, and the driver sitting high up behind, holding the reins over the roof. The statesman Benjamin Disraeli is said to have called it the gondola of London.",
"The horses stand patiently in the shafts, with thick woollen rugs over their backs to keep off the damp. One of them has a nosebag on, and is quietly eating its oats, its ears turning now and then towards a sound in the fog. Every so often it breathes out through its nostrils, and two little clouds of steam rise into the lamplight and slowly disappear.",
"Beside the rank stands a small wooden hut, painted dark green, with a pitched roof and windows all along its sides. Warm light shines through the glass, and a thin stove pipe on the roof breathes out a wisp of smoke. This is a cabmen's shelter, one of the first of its kind in London, put up so that cab drivers waiting for a fare have somewhere warm to sit.",
"The first shelters were built only a few years ago, by a charity set up in eighteen seventy five. Until then, a cab driver on a cold night had nowhere to go but a public house, and could not leave his horse for long. The shelters were built on the roadway itself, and so, by the rule of the police, each one could be no bigger than a horse and cab standing in the street.",
"Inside, about a dozen men can sit around a narrow table, and a man in an apron looks after a little stove, a kettle and a frying pan. He sells mugs of hot tea and coffee, bread and butter, rashers of bacon and chops, all at a fair price, and the cab drivers can bring their own food to be cooked. There are rules, too. No drinking of spirits, no gambling, and no swearing.",
"You cannot go inside, because the shelters are only for the cabmen. But the door opens for a moment as a driver comes out, wiping his mouth, and you catch a breath of warm air, smelling of tea, frying bacon and damp wool. Inside you glimpse men in thick coats, one reading a newspaper, one cutting bread, another asleep with his chin on his chest.",
"In the roof of each hansom, just in front of the driver's seat, there is a small trapdoor. The passenger, sitting snugly inside behind folding wooden doors, can push it open to speak to the driver, and pay the fare through it at the end of the journey. On a foggy night, a passenger may sit there with the trap open, giving directions street by street.",
"The driver climbs up into his high seat at the back of his cab, settles his rug over his knees, and takes up the reins. Somewhere along the rank a passenger has hailed him from the fog. He clicks his tongue softly, and the horse leans into its collar, and the cab rolls away, its two lamps swaying a little, until the fog closes around it.",
"From somewhere in the west, the clock at Westminster chimes again through the fog, the full eight notes of the half hour, slow and blurred. A cab driver standing by the shelter door takes out his watch, looks at it, and puts it back in his pocket with a satisfied nod, as though the clock had only just confirmed what he already knew.",
]),

dict(title="Hot Chestnuts and the Muffin Man", scene="cabstand", paras=[
"At the corner, near the shelter, there is a chestnut seller with a little iron brazier on legs. The coals glow orange through the holes in its sides, and on top sits a flat, perforated pan full of chestnuts, their shells split and blackened at the edges. The man turns them slowly with his hands, which seem not to feel the heat at all, and every now and then one of them cracks softly.",
"You stop and buy a pennyworth. He scoops them up and twists them into a little cone of paper, and they are almost too hot to hold. You peel one with your fingertips. Inside it is pale gold and soft and floury, and it tastes faintly sweet and smoky. You stand beside the brazier for a while, with the warmth of the coals on your face, eating them one by one.",
"A little way along, there is another stall that you can find by its smell alone. It is the baked potato man, with a tall, shining tin can on legs, heated inside by a small charcoal fire, with a little chimney poking out of the top. In the oven at its heart, potatoes are kept hot. He splits one open for a customer, adds butter and a pinch of salt, and wraps it in paper.",
"Writers who described London's streets in these years said that people often bought hot potatoes not only to eat, but to warm their hands on the way home, and then ate them when they had gone cold. On a night like this, you can understand why. A young clerk walks off down the street holding one in each hand inside his coat pockets, like two little stoves.",
"And then, through the fog, you hear a bell. It is not the clock this time, but a small handbell, rung slowly as someone walks along. Out of the fog comes the muffin man, carrying a wide wooden tray on his head, covered with a cloth to keep in the warmth. Under the cloth are rows of soft round muffins, ready to be toasted by the fire at home.",
"A door opens on the other side of the street, and a maid comes hurrying down the steps with a plate. The muffin man stops, lifts the tray down from his head, folds back the cloth, and counts out half a dozen muffins onto her plate. They exchange a few words about the fog, and she says she can hardly see her own hand in it, and then she hurries back inside.",
"You decide to buy two for yourself, for later. The muffin man wraps them for you in a piece of paper, warm and soft, and you tuck them carefully into your coat pocket. Then he settles the tray back on his head, picks up his bell, and walks slowly away down the street, its gentle ringing growing fainter and fainter until it is just a little shimmer of sound in the fog.",
"Down near the river, a coffee stall is being set up for the night, a painted wooden cart with a big copper urn on top and a lamp hanging from its roof. Stalls like this stay open until the early morning, for the late workers of the city. The stall keeper sets out thick white mugs and slices of bread and butter, and a cab driver is already waiting, rubbing his hands.",
"Further along the street, a policeman is walking his beat, in a long cape glistening with damp, and a tall helmet. At his belt hangs a lantern with a round glass lens in front, called a bull's eye, which can throw a beam of light where he points it. He walks at the same slow, steady pace he always walks, trying the handles of shop doors as he passes, to be sure they are locked.",
]),

dict(title="The Quiet Square", scene="fogstreet", paras=[
"Now you turn away from the busy streets, and walk up into the quieter part of the town, where you live. The roads grow narrower, and the sounds of the city fall away behind you. Your own footsteps on the wet pavement are the loudest thing you can hear, and even they seem to be absorbed by the fog almost as soon as you make them.",
"Here and there, at a crossing, a boy is standing with a lighted torch, made of a stick wrapped in rags dipped in pitch. On the very thickest nights of fog, boys like this come out to guide people through the streets for a penny, walking ahead with the flame held high. These are called link boys, and their trade is very old, from the days before streets had lamps at all.",
"At the edge of a muddy crossing stands an old man with a broom, a crossing sweeper. All day long he sweeps a clean path across the road, through the mud and the straw and the leavings of the horses, so that ladies and gentlemen can cross without spoiling their shoes and hems, and they give him a coin if they are kind. Tonight he leans on his broom in the fog, nodding to you as you pass.",
"You come out at last into a small square, which is your square. In the middle there is a garden behind iron railings, where a few tall plane trees stand with their branches bare, and fallen leaves lie in the grass. The garden is locked at night, and only the people who live around the square have keys. It is very still in there, a little island of darkness inside the fog.",
"The plane trees of London have been planted in squares and along streets all through the century, partly because they are so tough. Their bark peels away in patches, so that the soot of the city does not choke them, and underneath there is fresh, pale bark, cream and green and grey, like the pattern on an old map. In the fog, their trunks look almost silver.",
"The houses around the square are quieter and a little grander than the ones by the river. Most of the windows are lit, behind curtains or blinds. From one of them, on the first floor, comes the sound of someone playing the piano, softly, a slow tune you almost recognise, the notes coming out muffled through the glass and the fog. You walk slowly past, listening.",
"On the corner of the square, a gas lamp stands over the steps of the house where you have your rooms. Your landlady keeps a respectable house, and on foggy evenings she leaves a lamp burning in the hall, so that her lodgers can find the keyhole. You climb the four steps, feeling for each one with your foot, and fit your key into the lock.",
"As you open the door, the clock at Westminster chimes one more time, far away across the rooftops, a faint and wavering sound. It is the hour now, and after the long chime of sixteen notes, the great bell strikes slowly, each deep note rolling out over the fog and dying away. You count them in your head, as you always do, and then you step inside and close the door.",
"Inside, the hall smells of beeswax polish and coal smoke and a little of supper. The gas is turned down low. A coat stand holds the other lodgers' coats, still damp, and an umbrella stand holds their umbrellas. From the kitchen below comes the comfortable clink of dishes being washed. You take off your wet coat and hang it up, and climb the stairs to your own room.",
]),

dict(title="By the Parlour Fire", scene="parlour", paras=[
"Your room is on the first floor, at the front of the house, overlooking the square. The landlady's maid has already lit the fire for you, and it is burning well in the black iron grate, the coals glowing red and orange between the bars, with little blue flames dancing along the top. A brass fender stands in front of it, shining softly in the firelight.",
"You turn up the gas a little. On the wall beside the fireplace there is a bracket with a glass shade, shaped like a tulip, and as you turn the little tap, the flame inside it grows brighter, filling the room with a warm, slightly yellow light. Then you turn it down again, low, because the firelight is enough, and the room feels more peaceful in its glow.",
"The room is crowded in the comfortable way of the time. There is a round table with a fringed cloth, an armchair by the fire with a crocheted cover on its back, called an antimacassar, to keep the hair oil of its sitter off the upholstery. There is a bookshelf, a little writing desk, and on a stand by the window, an aspidistra, the tough green houseplant that grows happily in every dim, gaslit parlour in London.",
"On the mantelpiece, under a glass dome, there is a small clock, and beside it a pair of china dogs, a few letters tucked behind a candlestick, and a picture postcard of the sea. On the wall, a round clock with a long pendulum ticks slowly, the brass disc of its pendulum swinging back and forth behind a little window, catching the firelight on every swing.",
"On the little table beside the armchair there is a book waiting for you, the second volume of a long novel, borrowed from a circulating library. For a small yearly fee, these libraries lend out new books, usually in three volumes, one at a time, so that a reader may wait several days, and wonder, before the next part of the story arrives. You open it to your bookmark, and then put it aside for now.",
"Now it is time for the muffins. You take the long toasting fork from its hook beside the grate, split one muffin carefully with your fingers, and hold the halves out towards the glowing coals. Slowly the surface turns golden, and then a little brown at the edges. The smell of warm toasted bread fills the room. You butter them while they are hot, and the butter melts into the holes.",
"The kettle has been sitting on the hob at the side of the grate, and now it begins to sing softly, a thin column of steam curling from its spout. You make yourself a cup of cocoa, stirring the dark powder into the hot water with a little sugar and a splash of milk. Cocoa has become a popular evening drink in these years, warm and gentle, and sold as a wholesome, nourishing thing to drink before bed.",
"You sit down in the armchair, with your cocoa in one hand and a plate of muffins on your knee, and stretch your feet out towards the fender. Your boots are still a little damp, and soon you can see a faint steam rising from them too. The landlady's cat, a large tabby with white paws, has crept in through the door behind you, and now settles on the hearthrug with a sigh.",
"Outside, beyond the window and the lace curtains, the fog presses softly against the glass. The street lamp in the square makes a big, blurry halo of gold in it, and nothing else can be seen at all. It feels as if this room were the only room in the world, warm and lit and small, floating in a great soft cloud. The fire shifts. The clock ticks. The cat purrs.",
]),

dict(title="Goodnight from London", scene="parlour", paras=[
"When the cocoa is finished, you take the candlestick from the mantelpiece, light the candle from the fire with a twist of paper, and turn the gas right down until it goes out with a tiny sigh. In the next room your bed is waiting, and the maid has already put a stone hot water bottle between the sheets, so that the bed is warm right down to the bottom.",
"You undress by the candlelight and climb into bed, and pull the heavy blankets up around your shoulders. The sheets are cool at the edges and warm in the middle, where the bottle has been. Through the open door, you can still see the red glow of the parlour fire, settling slowly into embers. Let your body sink down into that warmth now, and let your arms and legs grow heavy.",
"Far away across the city, the lamplighter has finished his round and gone home to his own fire. The cab horses are dozing under their rugs. The chestnut seller has covered his brazier. The muffin man's bell is silent, and the policeman walks his slow beat along the quiet streets. All over London, behind millions of lit windows, people are settling down for the night, just as you are.",
"Let your breath slow down, until it is as soft and as even as the ticking of the parlour clock. In and out. In and out. If a thought comes to you, let it drift away into the fog like the sound of the muffin man's bell, growing fainter and fainter, until it is gone. There is nothing more to do tonight. Everything is done.",
"And while you sleep, the city sleeps too. Some time before morning, perhaps, a little wind will come up the river from the sea, and the fog will begin to thin, and lift, and drift away over the rooftops. By the time the lamplighter comes round again to put out the lamps, the sky may be pale and clear, and the first carts will rattle into the square. But that is a long, long way off yet.",
"Thank you for walking home through the fog with The Quiet Archive tonight. The candle is out, the fire is low, and the great clock at Westminster will keep watch over the city until morning. Sleep well. Goodnight.",
]),
]
